taking bronze, followed by " [:a {:href "/athletes/45021"} "Lisa Curry"] ". Caulkins’ time of 2:12.64 was the fourth fastest performance ever, although three weeks later East German " [:a {:href "/athletes/47471"} "Ute Geweniger"] " won the Friendship Games in 2:11.79, close to her world record of 2:11.73, set in 1981."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Caulkins never competed again, as " [:em {} "Swimming World"] " noted, “The woman many consider to be the greatest female swimmer in history had left the pool.” She married Australian swimmer " [:a {:href "/athletes/45295"} "Mark Stockwell"] ", who she met in Los Angeles, and they settled in Australia. Nancy Hogshead also retired after Los Angeles. The Duke University grad attended law school, and later became an outspoken proponent for women’s sports."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "B Final"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "243"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/50983"} "Tracy Caulkins"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} "2:14.47 (1 h4)"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "2:12.64 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "247"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/51059"} "Nancy Hogshead"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} "2:16.29 (1 h3)"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "2:15.17 (2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {:class "bib"} "17"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/45109"} "Michelle Pearson"]] [:td 